= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Heisenberg equation of motion> and the <spin commutation relations> give, componentwise,
$$
\frac{dS_i}{dt}=\frac{i}{\hbar}[-\gamma B_jS_j,S_i]
=-\gamma\epsilon_{ijk}B_jS_k.
$$
Thus $\boxed{d\mathbf S/dt=\gamma\mathbf S\times\mathbf B}$. For constant <magnetic field> this is <Larmor precession>: the component along $\mathbf B$ is conserved and the transverse components rotate. In particular, for $\mathbf B=B\widehat{\mathbf z}$,
$$
S_x(t)=S_x(0)\cos\gamma Bt+S_y(0)\sin\gamma Bt,\quad
S_y(t)=S_y(0)\cos\gamma Bt-S_x(0)\sin\gamma Bt,\quad S_z(t)=S_z(0).
$$
The signed <angular velocity> is $-\gamma\mathbf B$; the positive precession rate is $|\gamma B|$.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Here the dimensionless <spin one-half> operator is $S_x=\sigma_x/2$. Since the <Pauli matrix> obeys $\sigma_x^2=I$, the even and odd terms of the <matrix exponential> give
$$
e^{-i\theta S_x}=\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^m(\theta/2)^{2m}}{(2m)!}I
-i\sum_{m=0}^{\infty}\frac{(-1)^m(\theta/2)^{2m+1}}{(2m+1)!}\sigma_x
=I\cos\frac\theta2-i\sigma_x\sin\frac\theta2.
$$
This is the <exponential of a Pauli vector> for an $x$-axis <rotation>. With a physical <spin angular momentum> $S_x^{\rm phys}=\hbar\sigma_x/2$, the exponent is $-i\theta S_x^{\rm phys}/\hbar$.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Use the ordered <orthonormal basis> $(|1s\rangle,|2p_x\rangle)$. The projected <Hamiltonian> between pulses is $H_0=-\Delta\sigma_z$, so its free <time-evolution operator> through a period is
$$
U_\Delta=e^{i\alpha\sigma_z}=\begin{pmatrix}e^{i\alpha}&0\\0&e^{-i\alpha}\end{pmatrix},\qquad \alpha=\frac{\Delta\tau}{\hbar}.
$$
Across one instantaneous pulse, integrating the <Schrödinger equation> gives
$$
U_E=e^{-i\beta\sigma_x}=\begin{pmatrix}\cos\beta&-i\sin\beta\\-i\sin\beta&\cos\beta\end{pmatrix},\qquad \beta=\frac{Ex_0\tau}{\hbar},
$$
using the <exponential of a Pauli vector>. The finite free <Hamiltonian> contributes vanishing evolution during the zero-duration kick. A period measured immediately before successive pulses therefore consists of the kick followed by free evolution. Composition gives
$$
\boxed{U(N\tau-\epsilon,-\epsilon)=(U_\Delta U_E)^N,\qquad\epsilon\to0^+.}
$$
This is the <Floquet operator> at the specified phase of the pulse train. Actual hydrogenic <energy levels> are not opposite numbers: the displayed convention subtracts their midpoint, with $\Delta=(E_{2p}-E_{1s})/2$. Restoring that midpoint multiplies the result by an unobservable <global phase>. Likewise $E$ in the given <Hamiltonian> denotes the electric coupling per unit length; if it is instead a physical electric-field strength, its charge factor must be included in $Ex_0$.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Put $\alpha=\Delta\tau/\hbar$ and $\beta=Ex_0\tau/\hbar$. The <Pauli matrix multiplication law> gives the <pulse-and-precession spin one-half map>
$$
U_\Delta U_E=CI-i\mathbf v\cdot\boldsymbol\sigma,\quad C=\cos\alpha\cos\beta,\quad
\mathbf v=(\cos\alpha\sin\beta,-\sin\alpha\sin\beta,-\sin\alpha\cos\beta).
$$
Because $C^2+|\mathbf v|^2=1$, a suitable <angle> and <unit vector> for the <rotation> are
$$
\boxed{\theta=2\arccos(\cos\alpha\cos\beta),\qquad
\mathbf n=\frac{(\cos\alpha\sin\beta,-\sin\alpha\sin\beta,-\sin\alpha\cos\beta)}{\sqrt{1-\cos^2\alpha\cos^2\beta}}.}
$$
If the denominator vanishes, $U=\pm I$ and the axis is arbitrary. The <Bloch vector> $\mathbf r=\langle\boldsymbol\sigma\rangle$ rotates through $\theta$ about $\mathbf n$ per period; after $N$ periods the angle is $N\theta$. The eigenphases equivalently give <quasienergies> $\pm\hbar\theta/(2\tau)$ modulo $2\pi\hbar/\tau$.

In the two-orbital subspace the position operator is $X=x_0\sigma_x$. The <Heisenberg equation of motion> between pulses gives $P/m=(i/\hbar)[H_0,X]=(2\Delta x_0/\hbar)\sigma_y$, hence
$$
\langle X\rangle=x_0r_x,\qquad \langle P\rangle=\frac{2m\Delta x_0}{\hbar}r_y.
$$
Thus two components of the <Bloch vector> directly describe the mean <position> and <momentum>; its third gives the orbital population imbalance. The electric kick rotates about the position axis, leaving $\langle X\rangle$ unchanged at the kick, while free evolution mixes the position and momentum components. Starting in $|1s\rangle$, one complete period gives
$$
\mathbf r=(-\sin2\alpha\sin2\beta,-\cos2\alpha\sin2\beta,\cos2\beta),
$$
which exhibits the induced motion explicitly. These are projected two-orbital <observables>.

