= a
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume a real, central, short-range potential and elastic <quantum scattering>. At large <radius>, normalize the stationary <wavefunction> as
$$
\psi(\mathbf r)\sim e^{ikz}+f(\theta)\frac{e^{ikr}}r,
$$
where $k=\sqrt{2mE}/\hbar$ and $\theta$ is the <scattering angle>. Equal incoming and outgoing speeds give the <differential scattering cross-section> $d\sigma/d\Omega=|f(\theta)|^2$.

The <partial-wave scattering> expansion separates the <spherical harmonics>. The incident <plane wave> has expansion $\sum_{\ell\ge0}i^\ell(2\ell+1)j_\ell(kr)P_\ell(\cos\theta)$, with <spherical Bessel functions> $j_\ell$ and <Legendre polynomials> $P_\ell$. The regular radial solution outside the potential has asymptotic form $u_\ell\propto\sin(kr-\ell\pi/2+\delta_\ell)$. Its real <scattering phase shift> $\delta_\ell$ changes the ratio of outgoing to incoming waves to the <partial-wave S-matrix> $S_\ell=e^{2i\delta_\ell}$. Matching outgoing coefficients yields
$$
\boxed{f(\theta)=\frac1{2ik}\sum_{\ell=0}^{\infty}(2\ell+1)(S_\ell-1)P_\ell(\cos\theta)
=\frac1k\sum_{\ell=0}^{\infty}(2\ell+1)e^{i\delta_\ell}\sin\delta_\ell P_\ell(\cos\theta).}
$$
Here $\ell$ labels the <orbital angular momentum>, with squared magnitude $\hbar^2\ell(\ell+1)$. The assumption of purely elastic scattering is what makes every $S_\ell$ have unit <modulus>.

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Integrate the squared <partial-wave expansion of a scattering amplitude> over the solid angle. <Legendre polynomial> orthogonality eliminates terms with unequal angular-momentum indices, giving
$$
\sigma_{\rm tot}=2\pi\int_{-1}^1|f(\theta)|^2\,d(\cos\theta)
=\frac{4\pi}{k^2}\sum_{\ell=0}^{\infty}(2\ell+1)\sin^2\delta_\ell.
$$
At zero <scattering angle>, $P_\ell(1)=1$ and $\operatorname{Im}(e^{i\delta_\ell}\sin\delta_\ell)=\sin^2\delta_\ell$. Consequently
$$
\operatorname{Im}f(0)=\frac1k\sum_{\ell=0}^{\infty}(2\ell+1)\sin^2\delta_\ell,
\qquad\boxed{\sigma_{\rm tot}=\frac{4\pi}{k}\operatorname{Im}f(0).}
$$
This is the <optical theorem>. In the present elastic problem the total and elastic <cross-sections> coincide. For an absorptive interaction the inclusive total still obeys the <optical theorem>, but need not equal the integral of the elastic differential <cross-section>.

= c
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The radial <S wave> vanishes at the hard surface, so outside the sphere $u_0(r)=A\sin[k(r-R)]$. Comparison with $A\sin(kr+\delta_0)$ gives
$$
\boxed{\delta_0(k)=-kR\pmod\pi,\qquad a_0=-\lim_{k\to0}\frac{\tan\delta_0}k=R.}
$$
The <hard-sphere S-wave scattering> amplitude and <cross-section> are therefore
$$
\boxed{f_0(k)=-e^{-ikR}\frac{\sin(kR)}k,\qquad
\frac{\sigma_0}{4\pi R^2}=\left(\frac{\sin x}{x}\right)^2,\quad x=kR.}
$$
The three prominent features are the finite low-energy limit $\sigma_0\to4\pi R^2$, the oscillations with exact zeros at $x=n\pi$ for positive <integers> $n$, and the decreasing envelope $\sigma_0\le4\pi/k^2$. The zero-energy value is four times the geometric area $\pi R^2$. Near zero the normalized <cross-section> is $1-x^2/3+O(x^4)$. The large-$k$ fall here concerns the <S wave> alone; summing all <partial waves> gives a different high-energy total.

\Image[2019-q2-scattering.png]
{title=Hard-sphere S-wave cross-section and delta-shell Born angular scattering}
{description=Dimensionless curves calculated directly from the derived scattering amplitudes. The left panel concerns only the S wave, while the right panel shows different incident wave numbers for the weak delta-shell model.}

= d
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

In the <first Born approximation>, let $q=|\mathbf k'-\mathbf k|=2k\sin(\theta/2)$ be the transferred wave number. The angular <integral> in the three-dimensional <Fourier transform> is $4\pi\sin(qr)/(qr)$, so
$$
f_B(\theta)=-\frac{m}{2\pi\hbar^2}\int e^{-i\mathbf q\cdot\mathbf r}V_0\delta(r-R)\,d^3r
=-\frac{2mV_0R^2}{\hbar^2}\frac{\sin(qR)}{qR}.
$$
Writing $g=2mV_0R/\hbar^2$ and $\operatorname{sinc}u=\sin u/u$, with value one at zero, the <delta-shell Born amplitude> gives
$$
\boxed{f_B=-gR\operatorname{sinc}[2kR\sin(\theta/2)],\qquad
\frac{d\sigma_B}{d\Omega}=g^2R^2\operatorname{sinc}^2[2kR\sin(\theta/2)].}
$$
For $kR\ll1$ the scattering is approximately isotropic, with total <cross-section> $4\pi g^2R^2$. For $kR\gg1$ it has a forward lobe of angular width of order $(kR)^{-1}$ and oscillatory zeros where $2kR\sin(\theta/2)=n\pi$. At a fixed nonzero <scattering angle>, its envelope decreases as $k^{-2}$. The right-hand panel of the figure shows this angular dependence.

For real $V_0$ the first-order <scattering amplitude> is real, so inserting it literally in the <optical theorem> would predict zero total <cross-section>, contrary to its nonzero squared <modulus>. **The first Born amplitude alone does not satisfy the optical theorem at order $V_0^2$.** This is an order-of-approximation issue: the squared first-order amplitude is already second order, and the second Born term supplies the required second-order imaginary forward amplitude. At first order both sides vanish. A weak-shell regime is needed for the Born truncation itself to be accurate.

